  "report": "Project Overview and Goal Attainment\nThe Cheonggyecheon Restoration Project largely achieved its original goal by removing an aging elevated highway and restoring a 5.8-kilometer stream and public space through central Seoul. The project successfully improved the urban environment, created new pedestrian and recreational areas, and helped revitalize downtown Seoul.\n\nBeneficiaries and Excluded Groups\nThe greatest beneficiaries were Seoul residents, pedestrians, tourists, nearby property owners, and businesses that benefited from increased foot traffic and improved public space. However, some traditional merchants, manufacturers, street vendors, and small businesses near the project area were harmed by construction disruption, relocation pressures, and rising commercial rents.\n\nFinancial Sustainability and Housing Access\nThe project was primarily financed through the Seoul Metropolitan Government and required significant public investment, but its long-term economic and tourism benefits helped justify the cost. However, improving housing access was not a major goal of the project, and it did not significantly increase affordable housing opportunities for lower-income residents.\n\nDisplacement and Gentrification\nThe project contributed to rising land values and commercial rents in surrounding areas. Although large-scale residential displacement was not the central issue, some small businesses and traditional industries experienced relocation or economic pressure as the area became more attractive to tourists, investors, and higher-value commercial development.\n\nTransportation and Public Services\nRemoving the elevated highway initially raised concerns about severe traffic congestion, but Seoul expanded public transportation and introduced traffic-management policies to reduce car dependence. The project also significantly improved pedestrian access and provided a major new public recreational space in the center of the city.\n\nCommunity Support and Environmental Consequences\nPublic opinion became generally positive after completion because residents gained access to cleaner, greener public space. However, many local merchants initially opposed the project because they feared business losses and relocation. Environmentally, the project produced major benefits, including lower surrounding temperatures, improved air quality, increased biodiversity, and reduced the urban heat island effect.\n\nLessons for Future Governments\nFuture governments should copy Cheonggyecheon's strong investment in public space, environmental restoration, pedestrian infrastructure, and public transportation. However, they should avoid an overly top-down and rapid implementation process. Large urban regeneration projects should include stronger consultation with local residents and businesses, compensation programs, affordable commercial space, and anti-displacement protections from the beginning."
