  "report": "Project Overview and Goal Attainment\nSeoullo 7017 successfully transformed the aging Seoul Station Overpass, originally built in 1970, into an elevated pedestrian walkway and linear park that opened in 2017. Rather than demolishing the deteriorated structure, the Seoul Metropolitan Government reused it to create green public space and connect previously divided neighborhoods around Seoul Station. The project largely achieved its original goal of shifting the area from vehicle-centered infrastructure toward a more pedestrian-oriented urban environment.\n\nBeneficiaries, Harm, and Gentrification\nPedestrians, tourists, nearby businesses, and property owners around Seoul Station benefited the most from improved accessibility, public space, and increased visitor activity. The project also connected major destinations such as Namdaemun Market, Seoul Station, and surrounding neighborhoods. However, some local merchants and lower-income communities received fewer benefits and faced concerns about redevelopment pressure and rising property values. Although displacement was less severe than in projects such as New York's High Line, Seoullo 7017 demonstrated the risk that public-space improvements can contribute to gradual commercial gentrification.\n\nFinancial Sustainability and Housing Access\nThe project was primarily financed and managed by the Seoul Metropolitan Government as a public urban-regeneration investment. Reusing the existing overpass avoided completely replacing the structure while creating a new public amenity and tourism attraction. However, improving housing affordability was not a central objective, and the project did little to directly expand affordable housing or improve housing access for lower-income residents around Seoul Station.\n\nPublic Services, Transportation, and Community Reception\nSeoullo 7017 significantly improved pedestrian connectivity by linking the elevated walkway to multiple surrounding streets, transit facilities, cultural destinations, and commercial areas. It also introduced trees, planting areas, seating, and recreational facilities into a heavily developed part of central Seoul. Public reception became generally positive as the walkway developed into a recognizable attraction, although concerns existed about traffic changes, maintenance, accessibility, and the project's effects on nearby businesses. Environmentally, the project added urban greenery and pedestrian space, although its ecological impact was more limited than a full-scale environmental restoration project such as Cheonggyecheon.\n\nLessons for Future Governments\nFuture governments should replicate Seoullo 7017's adaptive reuse of aging infrastructure, pedestrian connectivity, preservation of urban history, and integration of green public space into dense city centers. However, they should avoid treating physical design alone as sufficient urban regeneration. Similar projects should include stronger affordable housing measures, protections for existing small businesses, continuous community participation, and careful planning for long-term maintenance and surrounding development."
